clearcote-956: carry the metric-compatible substitution fonts on macOS, so a cross-OS persona
has something to resolve to.

MEASURED on the native macOS arm64 build of 150.0.7871.114, canvas TextMetrics signature of
"WgQmil" at 32px against a guaranteed-absent family (document.fonts.check() answers true for
almost any name and is useless here):

                       platform='macos'   platform='windows'   a real Windows Chrome
  Segoe UI                 absent             absent              present
  Calibri                  absent             absent              present
  Consolas                 absent             absent              present
  Cambria / Candara         absent             absent              present
  Helvetica Neue           present            present              absent
  Lucida Grande / Menlo    present            ABSENT (correct)     absent

So 040-fonts was doing half the job on macOS: its same-OS and cross-OS hiding worked -- Lucida
Grande and Menlo were correctly gone under a Windows persona -- but not one Windows family was
ever substituted in, so the persona's font list was empty where a real Windows machine has
~230 families. An enumeration probe saw a machine with no Windows fonts claiming to be Windows,
which is a sharper signal than a browser that hides nothing.

WHY. MetricCompatibleSubstitute() returned "" for every host that is not Linux, on the
reasoning that "Windows/macOS hosts already have the real fonts". That holds for a Windows
host. It does not hold for a macOS host under a Windows persona: a stock macOS 27.0 machine
has one file in /Library/Fonts and none of Arimo, Carlito, Caladea, Tinos, Cousine, Selawik or
Gelasio. Unlike 971/1023 in the licensed line, this code is NOT in a Linux-only file -- it is
in the platform-neutral font_cache.cc and was compiled here all along; it just returned early.

WHAT THIS DOES. The clones in assets/fonts/ -- the same files the Linux release already
bundles, where the SDK points FONTCONFIG_FILE at fonts.conf.template to the same end -- now
also travel inside the macOS app bundle, at
<Chromium Framework.framework>/Resources/clearcote_fonts/, and are registered with CoreText at
kCTFontManagerScopeProcess the first time a cross-OS persona needs a substitute. Process scope:
visible to this process only, never written to the user's font database, gone when the process
exits; a launch with no persona, or a same-OS persona, registers nothing at all. The renderer
sandbox already allows reading the bundle (`(allow file-read* (subpath (param bundle-path)))`
in sandbox/policy/mac/common.sb), so no sandbox change is needed and none is made.

No new font files and no new licences: assets/fonts/ and its ATTRIBUTION.md are unchanged, and
scripts/provision-macos-fonts.sh copies them into the tree before gn gen, checked against
assets/fonts/SHA256SUMS so the macOS build stays reproducible from the public repo.
bundle_data sources are resolved at generation time, which is why provisioning has to happen
before gn gen rather than at package time.

ONE DIFFERENCE FROM LINUX, HANDLED. The kSubs table names more clones than assets/fonts/ ships
(the Noto script and symbol faces, IPAGothic, WenQuanYi Zen Hei, Lato, Oswald, Liberation Sans
Narrow). On Linux that is harmless, because fontconfig always answers with its best match. On
macOS CoreText answers nothing, which would report those families ABSENT where Linux reports
them present -- the two artifacts would disagree about the same persona. A substitute that
does not resolve therefore falls back to the sans clone, which is already what
MetricCompatibleSubstitute does for a family with no entry at all.

